This trip was sold to me by my mom as a trip to "hike up a mountain." Of course,
being a trail running enthusiast and an enjoyer of going up high places, I said yes.
Specifically, we are hiking up a part of the Yangmaiyong peak of the
[Daocheng Yading Nature Reserve](https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g1217767-d20942499-Reviews-Yading_Nature_Reserve-Daocheng_County_Garze_Tibetan_Autonomous_Prefecture_Sichu.html)
to go see the Milk Lake and the Five-Color Lake, and the highest altitude we are
reaching in this trip is about 4,700 meters above sea-level.

TripAdvisor describes the Daocheng Yading Nature Reserve as "a
high-altitude mountain sanctuary and Tibetan pilgrimage site centered on
three sacred snow-capped peaks," and reaches up to over 6,032 meters above
sea-level at its highest peak: Xiannairi. This is not the one we're hiking up
(thankfully) because high altitude means low oxygen, and low oxygen means a
harder time hiking. Although I've ~~run~~ power-hiked up other mountains at
lower altitudes, I think high-altitude hiking is still going to be a big jump
from my previous experiences since I'll be fighting with the incline _and_ the
lack of oxygen. Hopefully,  I'll feel that it was all worth it by the time I reach the lakes.

# Day 1: Bangkok to Kunming to Lijiang

The flight to Kunming was scheduled at 7:50AM. So, being the responsible adult
that my mom is, we had to arrive at the airport at around 5AM. This also means
that we had to wake up before 4AM on a Sunday to shower and take a taxi to the
airport since it takes about an hour to get there. Good news: We arrived in time!
Bad news: We now have about 2 hours to kill before boarding the plane because
the security was very fast!

Thankfully, my mom has access to a lounge as a benefit of one of her credit
cards (or something like that, I honestly don't know) so we (I) got to utilize
the lounge's facility (food offerings) to kill time. I had some fried rice, some
pastries, a can of ginger ale, and half a can of beer (my stomach was too full
of gas from the ginger ale to finish it). They weren't very good but they're edible.
Once it was time to board the plane, my mom and I just went to the gate and got
on the plane. There was no delay to the flight and after around 2.5 hours in the
sky, we safely arrived at Kunming Changshui International Airport at around
10:30AM local Chinese time.

One of the first things we had to do after landing is, of course, to go through
immigration. The immigration officer I got was interesting. He angrily spoke
Chinese to me like what I didn't understand the words that were coming out of
his mouth. Of course I don't understand any word that was coming out of his
mouth. I don't speak Chinese :D Eventually, he gave up and asked me in English
if I was there to travel or to work–to which I said for travel–and he let me in.

I get that there are many Thai people who speak Chinese very well given how
common Chinese-Thai people (คนไทยเชื้อสายจีน) are in Thailand. I'm assuming
that he thought I was one of them. To be fair, I am half Chinese on my mom's
side. However, I was mainly raised by the Thai half of my family so my Chinese
knowledge is limited to just the basic words–not enough to tell what travel and
business is. Anyway, now that we're done with immigration, the next step is to
go to the Kunming Railway Station.

Our tour guide had arranged for a van to come pick us up at the airport and take
us to the station. The car ride through Kunming city isn't that interesting in
my opinion. It's just a bunch of tall buildings and road decorations here and there.
It doesn't have the futuristic vibes of Shenzhen or Chongqing that I've seen on
the internet, giving more of an old town vibes than anything.

[]($image.asset('day1/kunming-cityscape-1.jpg'))

[]($image.asset('day1/kunming-cityscape-2.jpg'))

The train was scheduled for around 2PM, so we had a lot of time to spare. We got
some food on the plane but it wasn't enough to last until we arrive in Lijiang
in the evening. So, we had to go find some impromptu lunch inside the station.
My lunch was made up of a cup of instant noodles and a bottle of ginger ale.
For an instant noodle, it was pretty good. You can taste a bit of spice and
the richness of the broth. I'd recommend you give it a try. The ginger ale was meh.

[Noodles stir fry, yogurt, fruits, and some jelly on the plane]($image.asset('day1/plane-food.jpg'))

[Instant beef noodle and ginger ale at the station]($image.asset('day1/station-lunch.jpg'))

One interesting thing about the train station is that you actually have to go
through security before you can get to the gate and you have to scan your
passport before you get to the platform. It's just like an airport but for trains.
I don't think I've had to do anything like this when taking a train in Thailand
or Japan. I think that this is only the case for long-distance trains
(cross-city, etc.) but it's still kinda weird. All in the name of public safety
(and tracking your citizens), I guess.

We didn't have to wait long on the platform before we got on the train. The
train's interior actually resembles Japan's bullet train–just less organized.
There weren't enough storage space to store all the luggages so I had to leave
mine in the area between the carts. If this was the bullet train in Japan, I
think you'd need to buy a ticket for your large luggage and there's a limited
number of them. If you can't buy the storage space, then better luck next time!

[Train interior]($image.asset('day1/train-interior.jpg'))

[Train storage]($image.asset('day1/train-storage.jpg'))

It took about 4.5 hours to get from Kunming to Lijiang so we had a lot of time
to spare in the train. I opted to read a light novel I brought from home to save
on data usage and phone battery and made some good progress on it. I probably
went through a quarter of the book by the time we arrived. In case you're
interested, the light novel is [Danmachi: Familia Chronicle - Episode Ryuu](https://myanimelist.net/manga/103045/Dungeon_ni_Deai_wo_Motomeru_no_wa_Machigatteiru_Darou_ka__Familia_Chronicle_-_Episode_Ryuu)
where it covers a side story involving Ryuu saving a trafficked girl from a
casino owner and giving some backstory on her and the other waitresses at the
Hostess of Fertility. I bought this years ago and only thought to read it now. I
should've started earlier because it was pretty fun.

After we arrived at the Lijiang Railway Station, we met up with our guide
Noo-Dee and headed to Lavande Hotel Lijiang Ancient City South Gate Branch where
we will be staying for the night. Noo-Dee left us and returned home after taking
us to the hotel, so the group and I were on our own for dinner. We decided to
walk around the hotel and went into a beef noodle shop that had just opened.
After struggling to speak Chinese for a bit, I was able to place an order for a
bowl of (spicy) beef noodles and some dumplings. They were quite delicious.
They're not as spicy as they are red but they did get me sweating a bit.
Although they're spicy, I will say that it's not the kind of spice that is
spicy for spiciness' sake. It actually tastes like good spice if you know
what I mean. It's not spicy to torment you but the taste of heat actually makes
the dish better.

[Beef noodles]($image.asset('day1/dinner-noodles.jpg'))

[Dumplings]($image.asset('day1/dinner-dumplings.jpg'))

After dinner, we walked to Lijiang Old Town. It wasn't very interesting. In
fact, it doesn't even feel like an "old town." The shops inside "old town" are
very modern shops such as Starbucks, Chagee, 7-Eleven, Northface, etc. and I
have doubts about their existence when the old town was first built. We
ultimately decided to go back to the hotel because there was nothing "old" about
this old town. We would also found out later that part that we reached isn't the
real old town, but just a small part of it. We had to walk a bit further down to
see the _real_ old town, where we will visit on the later days.

